.TH EUCLID-CLI-EQS-GET-QUEUE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eqs-get-queue \- show one queue's definition and counters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eqs get-queue
.BR \-\-queue | \-q
.I NAME\fR|\fIERN
.SH DESCRIPTION
Prints one queue as JSON: its ERN, owner, visibility timeout, delay and retention period, its dead letter queue and retry limit, its tags, and how many messages are available, delayed and in flight.
.PP
A name is resolved in your session's account and namespace, so it means
your queue of that name and cannot reach another account's queue
called the same thing. A full ERN names one queue in the
installation and is what reaches another namespace's.
.PP
This is the same description
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-list-queues (1)
gives of each of its own, for a single queue. They share one
representation on purpose, so that a field added to a listing is in this
too rather than the two drifting apart.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-queue " " \fINAME\fR|\fIERN\fR
The queue. Required. A name is resolved in your session's account and
namespace; a full ERN reaches any namespace you are granted. See
.B NAMES AND ERNS
in
.BR euclid-cli-eqs (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The queue is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the server could not be reached, or no such
queue exists.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eqs get-queue --queue orders
euclid-cli eqs get-queue --queue ern:eqs:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:queue:orders
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eqs (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-list-queues (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-queue-ern (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-message (1)
